1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,004
General Thomas Wyatt had commanded a corps, run a
procurement directorate, and spent his

2
00:00:15,024 --> 00:00:21,233
retirement building Aegis Dynamics into the fourth
largest defence contractor in the United

3
00:00:21,253 --> 00:00:26,710
States. He was sixty-eight, he had a flag pin,
and he had asked for the meeting.

4
00:00:27,109 --> 00:00:32,718
He wanted a partnership, and he had a folder, and
the folder was about the six thousand

5
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:35,499
people in black on a ridge in
Pennsylvania.

6
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:40,818
Let's not dance. You've built a division. You've
built it inside a corporate shell with

7
00:00:40,838 --> 00:00:45,691
a state licence, which is legal, and you've done it
in a state where I know every member

8
00:00:45,711 --> 00:00:47,630
of the delegation, which was smart.

9
00:00:47,909 --> 00:00:52,644
What you can't do is use it. The moment you deploy
that formation anywhere that matters,

10
00:00:52,664 --> 00:00:57,723
you're a mercenary army, and this country has a hundred
and forty years of law about mercenary

11
00:00:57,743 --> 00:01:01,019
armies, and every one of those laws
will land on you at once.

12
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:04,290
And Aegis Dynamics solves that.

13
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:10,258
Aegis has held a Title 10 support contract since
2009. We can put your people under our

14
00:01:10,278 --> 00:01:15,974
flag, our licences, our congressional relationships.
You get to be useful. We get — let's

15
00:01:15,994 --> 00:01:18,480
call it access to your technology base.

16
00:01:18,879 --> 00:01:24,983
The narrator will note that this was a serious offer
made by a serious man, that it was

17
00:01:25,003 --> 00:01:31,388
the correct move from Wyatt's position, and that
Albert Wesker had been waiting for someone

18
00:01:31,408 --> 00:01:33,569
to make it for about two years.

19
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:37,320
General, may I show you something instead?

20
00:01:37,719 --> 00:01:40,210
That's usually how these things go wrong.

21
00:01:40,609 --> 00:01:44,700
Yes. Thursday. Bring whoever you like.

22
00:01:45,099 --> 00:01:51,830
Eleven defence ministers came, and four chiefs of
staff, and a Deputy Secretary, and General

23
00:01:51,850 --> 00:01:58,361
Thomas Wyatt of Aegis Dynamics, and forty-one members
of the international defence press,

24
00:01:58,381 --> 00:02:01,590
who were given cameras and told
to use them.

25
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:08,608
The narrator wishes to stress how strange that was.
Umbrella had never demonstrated anything

26
00:02:08,628 --> 00:02:14,886
military. It had never advertised the Security
Service. The invitation, hand-delivered,

27
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:21,019
said only: *A demonstration of a personnel
capability. No weapons will be displayed.*

28
00:02:21,419 --> 00:02:24,949
No weapons were displayed. That was
the entire point.

29
00:02:25,349 --> 00:02:31,156
Four hundred personnel stood in formation on the
field, in black, without rifles.

30
00:02:31,556 --> 00:02:35,921
The demonstration ran ninety minutes and
consisted of four things.

31
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:42,383
The first: they stood still. For thirty-one minutes, in
the rain that started at twenty-seven

32
00:02:42,403 --> 00:02:48,205
o'clock, without shifting weight, without adjusting a
strap, without a cough. A brigadier

33
00:02:48,225 --> 00:02:53,960
from a NATO member state timed it on his watch and
then stopped timing it because it had

34
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,726
become unpleasant to watch.

35
00:02:56,127 --> 00:03:01,955
The second: a live-fire lane, which the audience
could not see the end of, in which forty

36
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:07,540
personnel moved through twelve hundred metres of
woodland at night — the field lights

37
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:13,322
were killed for it — with no radio traffic
whatsoever, and cleared nineteen targets, and

38
00:03:13,342 --> 00:03:19,038
came back out having taken four simulated
casualties, and the four simulated casualties

39
00:03:19,058 --> 00:03:22,717
continued moving because they had not
been told to stop.

40
00:03:23,117 --> 00:03:29,296
The third was a question-and-answer session in which
HUNK, in a gas mask, answered technical

41
00:03:29,316 --> 00:03:35,225
questions from four generals for twenty minutes and
refused to answer any question about

42
00:03:35,245 --> 00:03:37,987
selection, training or medical standards.

43
00:03:38,388 --> 00:03:42,027
And the fourth was the one that ended
the defence industry.

44
00:03:42,427 --> 00:03:48,487
Wesker walked out onto the field in the rain and
stood beside a single soldier, a woman

45
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:54,497
of about thirty, and addressed the stands without a
microphone, and the narrator notes

46
00:03:54,517 --> 00:04:00,157
that his Presence was forty-six by then and that
the stands were entirely silent.

47
00:04:00,677 --> 00:04:08,467
This is Officer Dray. She has been employed by
Umbrella for four years. In that time she

48
00:04:08,487 --> 00:04:16,278
has not taken a day of sick leave, has not filed a
grievance, has not been late, and has

49
00:04:16,298 --> 00:04:21,248
not, on any occasion, required to be
told a thing twice.

50
00:04:21,528 --> 00:04:28,057
She costs approximately nine per cent of what a
soldier of an equivalent standard costs

51
00:04:28,077 --> 00:04:34,682
a national army over the same period, because she
does not require recruitment, does not

52
00:04:34,702 --> 00:04:41,532
require retention, does not require a pension, does
not require a family support structure,

53
00:04:41,552 --> 00:04:44,318
and cannot be lost to a better offer.

54
00:04:44,927 --> 00:04:52,317
I am not selling her. I want to be extremely clear about
that, because eleven of the gentlemen

55
00:04:52,337 --> 00:04:59,333
in that stand have already begun drafting a
requirement. Umbrella does not sell personnel

56
00:04:59,353 --> 00:05:01,697
and will never sell personnel.

57
00:05:01,978 --> 00:05:09,271
What I am telling you is that this exists. It exists
now, it is four hundred people on

58
00:05:09,291 --> 00:05:16,500
a field in Pennsylvania today and it will be six
thousand by Christmas, and it is not

59
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:20,668
for hire by any government on
earth at any price.

60
00:05:20,947 --> 00:05:27,425
(very cold) You are all, therefore, in a new
situation. I would encourage you to spend

61
00:05:27,445 --> 00:05:33,847
the next ten years thinking very carefully about
what kind of world you would like to

62
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:38,077
be in when somebody who is not me
builds the second one.

63
00:05:38,478 --> 00:05:41,848
He walked off the field. There were
no questions taken.

64
00:05:42,247 --> 00:05:48,286
Officer Dray stood on that field for ninety minutes in
the rain while eleven defence ministers

65
00:05:48,306 --> 00:05:53,765
looked at her, and the narrator has her file, and
it is four lines long and it is the

66
00:05:53,785 --> 00:05:56,408
most disturbing document in this chapter.

67
00:05:56,807 --> 00:06:04,000
*DRAY, K. Acquired 2036, Security Cohort, Absolute.
Four years' service. No sick leave.

68
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:08,478
No grievance. No disciplinary
record. No leave taken.*

69
00:06:08,877 --> 00:06:11,021
No leave taken. Four years.

70
00:06:11,420 --> 00:06:17,310
Wesker read that file on the aircraft home and the
Red Queen's predecessor logged what

71
00:06:17,330 --> 00:06:22,189
he said, which was nothing for eight
minutes, and then one instruction.

72
00:06:22,709 --> 00:06:30,461
Every Absolute employee of this organisation is to be
*placed on leave*. Not offered it.

73
00:06:30,481 --> 00:06:37,879
Placed. Four weeks a year, mandatory, enforced by
the roster, beginning immediately.

74
00:06:38,279 --> 00:06:44,297
It was the single largest administrative undertaking
in Umbrella's history at that point:

75
00:06:44,317 --> 00:06:50,335
fourteen thousand people who had never had a day off,
sent away for four weeks, with pay,

76
00:06:50,355 --> 00:06:53,659
with nowhere to go and nothing
they wanted to do.

77
00:06:54,060 --> 00:07:00,216
The Directorate's internal review of the first year,
in 2041, is a document the narrator

78
00:07:00,236 --> 00:07:04,990
would recommend to anybody who thinks this
story is about a company.

79
00:07:05,389 --> 00:07:10,893
Of fourteen thousand: nine thousand six hundred
spent the four weeks in their quarters.

80
00:07:10,913 --> 00:07:16,416
Two thousand one hundred travelled to a place their
file said they were from, looked at

81
00:07:16,436 --> 00:07:22,066
it, and came back early. Four hundred and eleven
asked, formally, in writing, to be given

82
00:07:22,086 --> 00:07:27,653
a task. Nineteen had what the medical staff called an
adjustment crisis and two of those

83
00:07:27,673 --> 00:07:28,860
required admission.

84
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:32,900
And one hundred and forty-one — one per
cent — did something.

85
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:39,127
The report lists them individually, which is
unusual, and the narrator suspects it was

86
00:07:39,147 --> 00:07:44,975
done at Wesker's instruction. A man who drove to the
Atlantic coast and sat on a beach

87
00:07:44,995 --> 00:07:50,890
for four weeks. A woman who bought a violin and
could not play it and kept it. Nine who

88
00:07:50,910 --> 00:07:56,806
went to the same town in Pennsylvania and did not
know why and found out, from a clerk,

89
00:07:56,826 --> 00:08:02,926
that it was in all their files as their birthplace,
and spent four weeks in a bar together

90
00:08:02,946 --> 00:08:06,530
working out that they had been made
in the same week.

91
00:08:07,050 --> 00:08:09,040
(very soft) One per cent.

92
00:08:09,439 --> 00:08:15,802
One per cent, Director. In four weeks. From a standing
start, with no model of what wanting

93
00:08:15,822 --> 00:08:16,713
something is.

94
00:08:17,113 --> 00:08:22,603
Then do it every year and let us find out
what the number is in twenty.

95
00:08:23,004 --> 00:08:28,915
It was twenty-six per cent in 2061. It was forty per
cent in 2081, the year before the

96
00:08:28,935 --> 00:08:34,985
first release cohort, and every single person on the
committee that designed the release

97
00:08:35,005 --> 00:08:40,297
programme cited that number as the reason they
believed it could work at all.

98
00:08:40,697 --> 00:08:44,944
The narrator will give you the consequences
in the order they arrived.

99
00:08:45,344 --> 00:08:51,377
Within nine days, the share prices of the four
largest defence contractors in the United

100
00:08:51,397 --> 00:08:57,499
States fell a combined thirty-one per cent, not
because Umbrella had entered their market

101
00:08:57,519 --> 00:09:03,691
but because it had demonstrated that the most
expensive component of modern military power

102
00:09:03,711 --> 00:09:09,538
— the trained, willing, motivated human being —
could be obtained by somebody outside

103
00:09:09,558 --> 00:09:10,913
the system entirely.

104
00:09:11,313 --> 00:09:17,494
Within four weeks, two European primes announced a
merger. Within four months a Congressional

105
00:09:17,514 --> 00:09:23,295
committee opened an inquiry into private military
capability that ran for two years and

106
00:09:23,315 --> 00:09:28,963
produced a statute that Umbrella had drafted
eighteen months earlier through Sloane's

107
00:09:28,983 --> 00:09:34,830
successor at the Agency, and that made Umbrella's
arrangement legal and everybody else's

108
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:35,364
illegal.

109
00:09:35,764 --> 00:09:38,844
And within a year, Aegis
Dynamics came apart.

110
00:09:39,244 --> 00:09:45,347
It was not sabotage. The narrator has checked this
carefully, because it is the moment

111
00:09:45,367 --> 00:09:49,264
Wesker's methods become
indistinguishable from weather.

112
00:09:49,663 --> 00:09:56,020
Aegis lost two programmes on cost, which happens;
lost a third to a European consortium,

113
00:09:56,040 --> 00:10:02,397
which happens; and then lost its chief technology
officer, its head of autonomy and four

114
00:10:02,417 --> 00:10:08,629
hundred engineers, all of whom went to Umbrella, all
of whom applied unsolicited, none

115
00:10:08,649 --> 00:10:10,368
of whom were approached.

116
00:10:10,769 --> 00:10:17,867
In April of 2041 Thomas Wyatt came back to Washington
and asked for another meeting, and

117
00:10:17,887 --> 00:10:20,699
this time he did not have a folder.

118
00:10:21,219 --> 00:10:25,009
I've got nine months of cash and a
board that wants to sell.

119
00:10:25,408 --> 00:10:26,379
Yes.

120
00:10:26,778 --> 00:10:27,908
You did this.

121
00:10:28,308 --> 00:10:35,529
No, General. I stood in a field in the rain and told
the truth about what a person costs,

122
00:10:35,549 --> 00:10:42,444
and your industry's entire business model was a bet
that nobody would ever say it out

123
00:10:42,464 --> 00:10:49,685
loud. I have not taken a contract from you. I have
not bid against you. I have not spoken

124
00:10:49,705 --> 00:10:51,718
to one of your engineers.

125
00:10:52,274 --> 00:10:59,538
I made you obsolete by existing, which is what I do,
and I did it in front of eleven defence

126
00:10:59,558 --> 00:11:04,844
ministers because I wanted it to happen
quickly rather than slowly.

127
00:11:05,244 --> 00:11:06,454
Why quickly?

128
00:11:06,854 --> 00:11:13,880
Because an industry that dies slowly spends its last
ten years lobbying, and an industry

129
00:11:13,900 --> 00:11:17,884
that dies quickly spends them
looking for a buyer.

130
00:11:18,283 --> 00:11:24,773
Umbrella acquired Aegis Dynamics in August 2041 for
eleven billion dollars, which was

131
00:11:24,793 --> 00:11:31,743
four per cent of what it had been worth in 2039, and
kept every engineer and every facility

132
00:11:31,763 --> 00:11:36,415
and closed every weapons programme it
owned within two years.

133
00:11:36,815 --> 00:11:42,964
The narrator will state that plainly, because it is
not what anybody expected: Albert

134
00:11:42,984 --> 00:11:49,134
Wesker bought the fourth largest arms manufacturer
in the United States and shut down

135
00:11:49,154 --> 00:11:50,005
its weapons.

136
00:11:50,404 --> 00:11:56,088
He kept the autonomy division, the materials
science, the space launch subsidiary that

137
00:11:56,108 --> 00:12:02,123
nobody had valued at anything, and the four hundred
engineers. He sold the munitions plants

138
00:12:02,143 --> 00:12:08,158
to the government at cost. He cancelled a missile
programme that was eleven billion dollars

139
00:12:08,178 --> 00:12:12,734
into a nineteen-billion-dollar contract and
paid the penalty himself.

140
00:12:13,884 --> 00:12:21,374
Industry absorbed: defence technology. Aegis Dynamics
acquired, fourteen weapons programmes

141
00:12:21,394 --> 00:12:28,389
terminated, space launch capability acquired.
Eighteen million Points. Note: you have

142
00:12:28,409 --> 00:12:35,568
removed fourteen weapons from this world and built
nothing to replace them. This is the

143
00:12:35,588 --> 00:12:42,252
fourth time I have recorded that sentence about
you. I have stopped expecting it.

144
00:12:42,844 --> 00:12:48,916
Wyatt was given an office with a view, a title with
the word *emeritus* in it, a salary

145
00:12:48,936 --> 00:12:55,009
he did not need and nothing whatsoever to do, which
is exactly what Graham Tolliver had

146
00:12:55,029 --> 00:13:01,032
been given fourteen years earlier in Trenton, and
which had by then become well enough

147
00:13:01,052 --> 00:13:04,254
known in American business that
it had a name.

148
00:13:04,653 --> 00:13:11,217
They called it *the Wilmington retirement.* It was
considered, by people who had watched

149
00:13:11,237 --> 00:13:18,025
four decades of hostile takeovers, to be unusually
humane, and it was discussed in business

150
00:13:18,045 --> 00:13:22,364
schools as evidence that Albert Wesker
was not vindictive.

151
00:13:22,764 --> 00:13:29,292
The narrator would like to offer a second reading,
which is Carrick's, from a memorandum

152
00:13:29,312 --> 00:13:31,674
of 2041 that was never answered:

153
00:13:32,193 --> 00:13:38,679
He doesn't destroy the men he beats. He keeps them,
on salary, in a building he owns,

154
00:13:38,699 --> 00:13:44,343
for the rest of their lives. Tolliver,
Halvorsen, now Wyatt. Nine of them.

155
00:13:44,624 --> 00:13:50,416
That isn't mercy. It's a *collection*. He is
assembling, one by one, every single person

156
00:13:50,436 --> 00:13:56,294
who ever stood in front of him, and he is keeping
them where he can see them, comfortable

157
00:13:56,314 --> 00:14:01,314
and useless and unable to complain about it
because he has been so generous.

158
00:14:01,594 --> 00:14:06,524
And the worst part is that they're grateful. I've
met four of them. They're grateful.

159
00:14:07,364 --> 00:14:08,987
He is not wrong.

160
00:14:09,386 --> 00:14:10,057
No.

161
00:14:10,456 --> 00:14:15,845
Does it trouble you that a man in your own
building can see you that clearly?

162
00:14:16,245 --> 00:14:23,242
It is the only reason I employ him. A man
surrounded exclusively by people who cannot

163
00:14:23,262 --> 00:14:30,260
disobey will, within about twenty years, become
incapable of distinguishing between a

164
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:32,655
good decision and a decision.

165
00:14:33,054 --> 00:14:40,296
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker said that in
2041, that it was entirely correct,

166
00:14:40,316 --> 00:14:44,264
and that he stopped acting on it
in about 2049.
